  "slug": "hall-quantum-4ded",
  "summary": "Seven preprints tie Hall-type and orbital responses to orbital degrees of freedom, disorder and interactions in 2D and 3D materials, including graphene and oxide interfaces.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is that Hall-type and related responses are traced to orbital character, quantum geometry, disorder and interactions rather than to spin magnetism or pristine band topology alone. Orbital-focused studies report defect-enhanced magnetoelectric responses in an orbital antiferromagnet, orbital Hall currents generated by disorder in amorphous systems including amorphous graphene, and intrinsic anomalous Hall conductivity from an orbital exchange field in spinless MoS2. Further work covers quantum-geometric nonlinear conductivity in SrTiO3 gases for (001) versus (111) interfaces, 3D quantum Hall responses where Chern numbers fail to characterize chiral behavior, and Bernal graphene with predicted anomalous Hall metals and measured hysteretic first-order transitions. The new element is treating disorder, defects and orbital or pseudospin order as active design variables for Hall responses.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
